<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 21 L3b. LOOP RECORDING: ONE TAKE PER CYCLE PASS (design D7), which
  is proposal 17's "phase 5" falling out of a verb that already existed rather
  than being built.

  Env, set on the CTest entry: SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_position.wav,
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_LATENCY_FRAMES=4800, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1; RUN_SERIAL.

  THE MECHANISM, so the numbers below are readable. The recorder does not
  "detect a wrap": the placement conversion is LINEAR in capture frame, so the
  pass a frame belongs to is arithmetic, `floor((placement - loopIn)/loopLen)`,
  and the recorder splits the take at those boundaries and emits ONE
  place-recording per segment, all at the loop start, inside ONE undo macro.
  place-recording then does the rest by its own proposal-17 rules: the first
  segment lands on an empty lane and becomes a plain cut; the second finds that
  cut covering the column and becomes a TAKE; the third likewise. So a lane
  ends with ONE COLUMN holding N takes, not N clips side by side, and that is
  what `clips="1"` plus `takes="3"` says.

  WHY THE ASSERTION IS A FLOOR AND NOT AN EXACT COUNT. How many passes a take
  produces is a WALL-CLOCK quantity ... captured material divided by the loop
  length ... and captured material shrinks when the box is loaded enough to
  cost ring overruns. An exact count is therefore load-sensitive, and this case
  DID fail that way under a concurrent suite before it was relaxed. So the
  count is a floor (7 s over a 2 s cycle leaves room for a second and a half of
  loss before 3 passes stops being true), and the part that is NOT
  load-sensitive is asserted instead, unconditionally, by the verb itself:
  ONE COLUMN, and exactly as many TAKES as there were PASSES. That pair is the
  whole claim of loop recording and it holds however many passes there were.

  The whole loop-record is ONE undo step, which is the property that makes it
  usable at all.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_loop_takes">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>

    <!-- The cycle region: 0 .. 2 s. set-property takes its value as a JSON
         ARRAY (SSetPropertyAction::readXml), hence the brackets. -->
    <set-property key="rangeStart" value="[0]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeEnd" value="[96000]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeValid" value="[true]"/>
    <cycle-enable/>

    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>

    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="7000"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- ONE column at the loop start, three takes, three committed passes,
         and the column is exactly the loop long. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="1" minTakes="3" minPasses="3"
                          startFrame="0" startTolerance="0"
                          durationFrames="96000" durationTolerance="0"
                          inputLatencyFrames="4800"
                          outputLatencyFrames="1024"
                          compensationFrames="-5824"/>

    <!-- ONE undo removes the whole pass. Nothing undoable may come between
         the take and this: a select-take probe here would be undone instead,
         which is how the first draft of this case mis-read a working undo as
         a broken one. -->
    <undo/>
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="0"/>

    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
